**Why this deviates from a literal "callout inline in the trigger":**

The contract itself is kept 1:1 with the ticket — **one `POST callout:ERP/api/customers` per new account**, body = a JSON array containing exactly that single customer (`salesforceId` / `name` / `country`, `country: null` when blank), `Content-Type: application/json`. Two platform facts force *where* the send runs:

1. **No callout is possible in the same transaction as the Account insert** (`System.CalloutException: You have uncommitted work pending`), so the send has to be deferred to an async transaction that starts after commit.
2. **100 callouts max per transaction**, so a marketplace batch of 200 must be spread over at least two async transactions.

In my first version the second chunk was obtained by **chaining a Queueable from `execute()`**, which blew up in the 200-record test with `System.AsyncException: Maximum stack depth has been reached`. The fix: the **trigger chunks the customers at enqueue time** (≤100 per chunk, i.e. ≤2 jobs for a batch of 200) and each job simply sends its requests — **no job ever enqueues another job**, so there is no async nesting/chain at all. The only observable trade-off remains that the push happens right after commit (async), which is unavoidable on this platform. Error handling/retries stay out of scope as specified.

