/**
 * Creates the "CSAT survey" follow-up Task for a single Case.
 *
 * One Case per job: the work is isolated, so a failure against one Case can
 * never prevent the survey from being scheduled for the others. Each execution
 * does exactly one SOQL query and one DML row, so governor limits are never a
 * concern even when hundreds of cases are closed at once - every case gets a
 * fresh set of limits in its own transaction.
 */
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    /**
     * Apex allows at most 50 enqueueJob calls in one transaction. One slot is
     * kept free for the continuation job, so at most 49 jobs are scheduled
     * directly per transaction (49 + 1 continuation = 50).
     */
    public static final Integer ENQUEUE_BUDGET = 49;

    /** Case Ids already handed to the queue in the current transaction. */
    private static final Set<Id> SCHEDULED_CASE_IDS = new Set<Id>();

    /** Normal mode: one Case -> one job -> one Task. */
    private Id caseId;

    /**
     * Continuation mode: only used when a transaction contains more newly closed
     * Cases than the platform allows to be enqueued at once (e.g. the 200-case
     * nightly cleanup).
     */
    private List<Id> pendingCaseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseId = caseId;
        this.pendingCaseIds = new List<Id>();
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseId = null;
        this.pendingCaseIds = new List<Id>();
        if (caseIds != null) {
            for (Id queuedId : caseIds) {
                this.pendingCaseIds.add(queuedId);
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * Returns true the first time a Case is scheduled in this transaction and
     * false for any repeat, so a Case can never get two surveys from one close.
     * The guard lives here (not in the trigger) because it must survive every
     * trigger invocation that happens inside the same transaction.
     */
    public static Boolean markScheduled(Id scheduledCaseId) {
        return scheduledCaseId != null && SCHEDULED_CASE_IDS.add(scheduledCaseId);
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseId != null) {
            createSurveyTask(caseId);
        } else {
            schedulePendingCases();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Schedules the pending Cases as one job each and, if they do not fit into
     * this transaction's enqueue budget, chains a continuation job with the rest.
     * Chain depth is 49 cases per hop, so thousands of cases still fit well
     * inside the platform's 50-level chain limit.
     */
    private void schedulePendingCases() {
        Integer enqueued = 0;
        Integer index = 0;

        while (index < pendingCaseIds.size() && enqueued < ENQUEUE_BUDGET) {
            Id nextCaseId = pendingCaseIds[index];
            index++;
            enqueued++;
            enqueueSafely(new CaseSurveyJob(nextCaseId), nextCaseId);
        }

        if (index < pendingCaseIds.size()) {
            enqueueSafely(new CaseSurveyJob(remainingFrom(index)), null);
        }
    }

    /** Apex List has no subList(), so the tail is copied out by index. */
    private List<Id> remainingFrom(Integer startIndex) {
        List<Id> rest = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = startIndex; i < pendingCaseIds.size(); i++) {
            rest.add(pendingCaseIds[i]);
        }
        return rest;
    }

    /** Never let a single scheduling failure stop the remaining cases. */
    private static void enqueueSafely(Queueable job, Id failedCaseId) {
        try {
            System.enqueueJob(job);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.debug(
                LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'CaseSurveyJob: could not schedule survey for ' +
                (failedCaseId == null ? 'the remaining cases' : failedCaseId) +
                ': ' + e.getMessage()
            );
        }
    }

    /** Builds the survey Task for one Case. The closed transition was already
     *  checked by the trigger, so the only business rule left here is: a Case
     *  without a Contact gets no Task. */
    private static void createSurveyTask(Id recordId) {
        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id = :recordId
            LIMIT 1
        ];

        // The Case was deleted (or never existed) between scheduling and running.
        if (cases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Case record = cases[0];

        // No contact -> no survey task.
        if (record.ContactId == null) {
            return;
        }

        insert new Task(
            WhatId = record.Id,
            WhoId = record.ContactId,
            Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + record.CaseNumber,
            ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
            Status = 'Not Started'
        );
    }
}
```

**Explanation of what I changed and why**

1. **Compile fix — `List.subList()` does not exist in Apex.** The continuation job needed the tail of `pendingCaseIds` after scheduling 49 cases. Replaced the call with a small `remainingFrom(Integer)` helper that copies the remaining elements into a new `List<Id>` by index. Also replaced the `new List<Id>(caseIds)` copy constructor in the constructor with an explicit copy loop, which is the unambiguous form.

2. **Compile fix — `Variable does not exist: CaseSurveyJob` in the trigger.** This was a cascade of the class failing to compile; with `CaseSurveyJob.cls` now valid, `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))`, `CaseSurveyJob.markScheduled(...)` and `CaseSurveyJob.ENQUEUE_BUDGET` all resolve.

3. **No SOQL/DML in the trigger.** It only compares `Trigger.old`/`Trigger.new` on `IsClosed` and enqueues, so bulk closes (list view or the 200-row nightly API job) cost no queries, no DML rows and no row-lock contention.

4. **50 `enqueueJob` limit.** Exceeding 50 `enqueueJob` calls in one transaction throws an *uncatchable* `System.LimitException` that would roll back the whole nightly close. The trigger still enqueues **one job per case** for the first `ENQUEUE_BUDGET = 49` cases and hands the rest to a continuation `CaseSurveyJob(List<Id>)`, which schedules them one job each and chains itself with whatever still doesn't fit (49 + 1 chain = 50 per transaction, ≈ 4 hops for 200 cases, far below the 50-level chain limit). For up to 49 newly closed cases the behaviour is exactly the naive version. Isolation is preserved — the continuation only schedules, it never does survey work itself.

5. **`markScheduled` guard.** A transaction-scoped static `Set<Id>` in `CaseSurveyJob` guarantees one survey per case even if the trigger fires again for the same case later in the same transaction, and it lives in the class so the state definitely survives across trigger invocations.

6. **Contact check in the job.** Per the spec the job decides; a case without a contact returns without inserting anything. The trigger schedules every newly closed case so the rule is enforced in one place (and also covers the contact being removed before the job runs).

7. **`with sharing`, one SOQL, one DML.** `with sharing` keeps the job inside the closing user's record access, so `Task.OwnerId` defaults to the agent who closed the case and `Date.today()` uses their time zone. No "task already exists" query is added: re-closing a reopened case should produce a fresh survey, and the trigger guard already prevents duplicates within a transaction.

